Day 17
-------

Wrapper classes
---------------
A Wrapper Class is a class that wraps (converts) a primitive data type into an object.

In Java, primitive data types like int, char, double, etc., are not objects. Wrapper classes allow these primitive values to be used where objects are required.

int --->Integer
float--->Float
double--->Double
char--->Character
boolean--->Boolean


NOTE: only  primitive classes have wrapper class 

1)String--->int      (converting string to int) Integer.parseInt(String value)
2)String---->double   (converting string to double)  Double.parseDouble(String value)
3)String--->bool     (converting string to boolean) boolean.parseBoolean(String value)
4)String--->char     Not possible



any datatype---->string
String.valueOf() --->convert all premitive data types into string format.

When we work with collections we are using collection classes.
ArrayList arr=new ArrayList();

ArrayList<Integer> arr=new ArrayList<Integer>(); //valid

ArrayList<int> arr=new ArrayList<int>(); //not valid


Packages & Access Modifiers
1)built-in packages
2)user defined packages

Access modifiers: defines scope of variables and methods
-----------------

1)public - A public member can be accessed from anywhere in the program.
2)protected - outside the package we can access but only through inheritance
3)default - Access only within the same package
4)private - Access only within the class not outside other class

